  <h2>The four parts of a useful note</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>The source, precisely:</strong> paper, page, and the passage itself.</li>
    <li><strong>The idea, in your words:</strong> one per note, so it can be moved and combined.</li>
    <li><strong>Whose it is:</strong> the paper's claim, your interpretation, or something a tool generated.</li>
    <li><strong>Why it matters:</strong> which question or chapter it serves.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>A workflow that holds up over a PhD</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Highlight while reading, but write a note only when you have a thought about the passage.</li>
    <li>Colour-code consistently, for example yellow for claims, green for methods, blue for numbers you might reuse.</li>
    <li>Keep project folders or workspaces, and put each paper in every project it serves.</li>
    <li>Before writing a section, read the notes for that project, not the papers again.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Highlighting everything and noting nothing.</li>
    <li>Notes in a separate app that lose the link to the page.</li>
    <li>Mixing AI summaries with your own notes until you can't tell them apart, and missing an <a href={href('glossary/hallucination/')}>AI hallucination</a> as a result.</li>
  </ul>
